<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">State of Xu (Huaiyi)</h1><p class="st-meta">1000 BCE – 512 BCE (approx.) · capital Xucheng</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The leading Huaiyi state of the lower Huai River, whose rulers called themselves kings and challenged the Zhou, before Wu destroyed it in 512 BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Xu was ruled by a lineage of the Ying clan among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huaiyi">Huaiyi</a> peoples of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangsu">Jiangsu</a> and Anhui, with its centre near modern Sihong. It resisted the Zhou from the early <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/western-zhou">Western Zhou</a>, and bronze inscriptions record royal campaigns against the Huaiyi and Xu.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Unlike most Zhou states, Xu's rulers used the title of king, and inscribed bronzes of the 'kings of Xu' have been found across the Huai and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> region. Later tradition celebrated King Yan of Xu as a benevolent ruler who gave up war rather than see his people suffer. Xu was a cultural bridge between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-plain-china">Central Plain</a> and the emerging states of the south-east.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Squeezed between Chu and the rising <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-wu">State of Wu</a>, Xu was conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-helu-of-wu">King Helü of Wu</a> in 512 BCE, and its ruler fled to Chu. Xu culture influenced the bronze art of Wu and Yue, and the surname Xu is traced to the state.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1000 BCE – 512 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:39.80%;width:9.71%"></span>